    "id": "mijikenda-werner-1915-nicolle-2016-digo",
    "title": "Kaya Age-Grades (Nyere, Khambi, Vaya) & Digo ([dig] E73) Information Structure across 11 Eastern Bantu Languages (Werner 1915 & Nicolle 2016, Ch. 20, pp. 377–395)",
    "summary": "Prof. Alice Werner (1915) documents the nine Mijikenda Kaya age-grades and sacred mwanza friction-drum, while Steve Nicolle (2016, Ch. 20, pp. 377–395) analyzes left/right dislocation of topics, clefted subject argument focus, and VS thetic sentences in Digo ([dig] E73, Kwale District, Kenya) and 10 other Eastern Bantu languages.",
    "content": "From Prof. Alice Werner (*\"The Bantu Coast Tribes of the East Africa Protectorate\"*, JRAI Vol. 45, 1915, pp. 326–354) and Steve Nicolle (*\"Variation in the expression of information structure in eastern Bantu languages\"*, in Doris L. Payne, Sara Pacchiarotti & Mokaya Bosire, eds., *Diversity in African languages*, Berlin: Language Science Press, 2016, Ch. 20, pp. 377–395, DOI: `10.17169/langsci.b121.491`):\n1. **The Nine Coastal *Kaya* Settlements, Age-Sets & the Sacred *Mwanza* Drum (Werner 1915, pp. 327–345)**:\n   - Documents the fortified hilltop *kaya* settlements of the *Wanyika* (Mijikenda: Giriama, Rabai, Duruma, Digo, Chonyi, Jibana, Kambe, Ribe, Kauma), their migration from *Shungwaya*, the generational *rika* cycles, the elder council grades **Kambi** and **Vaya** (custodians of the sacred *fisi* hyena oath and the friction-drum **mwanza**), and the carved wooden memorial posts (**vigango**).\n2. **Information Structure & Constituent Order in Digo (`[dig]` E73, Kwale District, Kenya, 864 clauses) & 10 Eastern Bantu Languages (Steve Nicolle 2016, Ch. 20, pp. 377–395, Table 1 & Ex. 1–30)**:\n   - **(1) Left Dislocation vs. Right Dislocation of Topics (§2, Ex. 2–3, 7–8, 11)**: Digo uses left-dislocation with an object prefix for object topics (`[Barabara]_TOPIC ndipho [a-ka-i-rich-a]_COMMENT`, Ex. 2) and topicalization without an object prefix when the verb is focused (`[Pesa]_TOPIC sino [hu-na-Ø-hew-a]_COMMENT`, Ex. 3); for subjects, Digo uses left-dislocation for switch topics (`[go mafuha ga taa]_TOPIC ndo [n’yarya?]_COMMENT`, Ex. 7) and for continued topics across a paragraph boundary (`Yuya mlume achiamba... Yuya bwana warima munda uchifika dza Mazera, na hiku uchifika dza Malindi ela kaguwire hata tsere mwenga`, Ex. 8), but uses **right-dislocation for continued topics within a paragraph** (`Lakini [achinyamala]_COMMENT [mutu yuyu]_TOPIC wala kayagomba na mutu`, Ex. 11, p. 383).\n   - **(2) Subject Argument Focus via Clefts (§3, Ex. 22)**: Unlike Jita, Kwaya, Kabwa, Rangi, Mwani, Bena, Malila, and Makonde (which place focused subjects post-verbally), Digo and Fuliiru prohibit post-verbal subject argument focus and require a **cleft construction**: `Ndipho atu achimanya kukala [iye]_FOCUS [ndi=ye ariyehenda mambo higo]_PRESUPPOSITION` ('That is when people knew that [it was] he [who did these things]', Ex. 22, p. 388).\n   - **(3) VS Thetic Sentences & Locative Existential Agreement (§4, Ex. 25)**: Digo allows only **VS constituent order** in thetic (presentational/all-new) clauses: `Hipho kare... Achiuka mwanache mmwenga achenda mwituni kutsonga ngoma. Phahi rero, chisha chindi, [ratuluka] [fisi], rina chitswa dza cha mutu, rina mikono minane na magulu manane, rina m’yo wa chuma` ('Once upon a time... One youngster went to the forest to carve a drum. Then today, after a short time, out came a giant hyena, having a head like a person's, having eight hands and eight legs, having an iron heart', Ex. 25, p. 390).",
