    "title": "Steve Nicolle (2016, Ch. 20, pp. 377–395, DOI: 10.17169/langsci.b121.491): 'Variation in the expression of information structure in eastern Bantu languages' — Complete Synthesis of Digo ([dig] E73, Kwale District, Kenya, 864 Clauses), Suba-Simbiti ([ssc] EJ403) & 9 Sister Languages",
    "summary": "Complete line-by-line documentation of Chapter 20 ('Variation in the expression of information structure in eastern Bantu languages', pp. 377–395, Table 1 & Ex. 1–30) by Steve Nicolle (Canada Institute of Linguistics & SIL International) in Doris L. Payne, Sara Pacchiarotti & Mokaya Bosire, eds., Diversity in African languages (Berlin: Language Science Press, 2016).",
    "content": "From Steve Nicolle (*\"Variation in the expression of information structure in eastern Bantu languages\"*, in Doris L. Payne, Sara Pacchiarotti & Mokaya Bosire, eds., *Diversity in African languages: Selected papers from the 46th Annual Conference on African Linguistics*, *Contemporary African Linguistics 1*, Berlin: Language Science Press, 2016, Chapter 20, pp. 377–395, DOI: `10.17169/langsci.b121.491`):\n1. **Table 1 Corpus Across 11 Eastern Bantu Languages (§1, p. 378)**:\n   - **Fuliiru (`[flr]` DJ63, DRC)**: 15 oral texts, 1,105 clauses (Van Otterloo 2011; 2015).\n   - **Digo (`[dig]` E73, Kwale District, Kenya / Tanga Region, Tanzania)**: 5 oral + 2 written texts, **864 clauses** (Nicolle 2013; 2015b).\n   - **Jita (`[jit]` EJ25)**: 6 oral + 4 written texts, 725 clauses (Pyle & Robinson 2015).\n   - **Kwaya (`[kya]` EJ251)**: 3 oral + 6 written texts, 417 clauses (Odom 2015).\n   - **Suba-Simbiti (`[ssc]` EJ403)**: 8 oral texts, **513 clauses** (Masatu 2015).\n   - **Kabwa (`[cwa]` EJ405)**: 9 oral texts, 710 clauses (Overton 2015).\n   - **Rangi (`[lag]` F33)**: 10 oral texts, 1,357 clauses (Stegen 2011).\n   - **Mwani (`[wmw]` G403)**: 9 oral texts, 286 clauses (Floor 2005).\n   - **Bena (`[bez]` G63)**: 10 oral + 5 written texts, 1,291 clauses (Eaton 2015).\n   - **Malila (`[mgq]` M24)**: 4 oral + 7 written texts, 838 clauses (Eaton 2015).\n   - **Makonde (`[kde]` P23)**: 8 oral texts, 610 clauses ( Leach 2015).\n2. **Parameter 1: Obligatory vs. Optional Left & Right Dislocation of Topics (§2, Ex. 1–12, pp. 379–384)**:\n   - **Objects in Digo (Ex. 2–3, pp. 379–380)**: Left-dislocated object topics trigger an agreeing object prefix on the verb (`Achiryagiza rirya tunda... [Barabara]_TOPIC ndipho [a-ka-i-rich-a]_COMMENT, achishika njira ya mwituni`, Ex. 2, p. 379), whereas topicalized objects omit the object prefix when the verb carries predicate focus (`[Pesa]_TOPIC sino [hu-na-Ø-hew-a]_COMMENT`, Ex. 3, p. 380).\n   - **Subjects in Digo (Ex. 7–8 & 11, pp. 381–383)**: Digo left-dislocates **switch topics** (`Pho mwanzo wa higo gosi ni kukala chapho kare Andago agulirwa mafuha ga taa ni Adigo, ela [go mafuha ga taa]_TOPIC ndo [n’yarya?]_COMMENT`, Ex. 7, p. 381) and **continued topics across a paragraph break** (`Yuya mlume achiamba, \"Haya, phahi n’richa n’kuambire gangu.\" Yuya bwana warima munda uchifika dza Mazera, na hiku uchifika dza Malindi ela kaguwire hata tsere mwenga`, Ex. 8, p. 382), but **right-dislocates continued topics within a paragraph** (`Lakini [achinyamala]_COMMENT [mutu yuyu]_TOPIC wala kayagomba na mutu`, Ex. 11, p. 383), contrasting with Rangi, Mwani, and Makonde where topical subjects must be obligatorily left-dislocated and post-verbal subjects are always focal (Ex. 9–10).\n3. **Parameter 2: Post-Verbal Subject Argument Focus vs. Obligatory Clefts (§3, Ex. 13–22, pp. 384–389)**:\n   - While Jita, Kwaya, Kabwa, Rangi, Mwani, Bena, Malila, and Makonde express subject argument focus via VS constituent order (e.g., Bena Ex. 13–14, Jita Ex. 15, Kabwa Ex. 17–18; Suba-Simbiti reverse pseudocleft `[Omoremo ghono yaamanyirë]_PRESUPPOSITION [no-bhötëghi ubhwa sinswe]_FOCUS` in Ex. 19), **Digo and Fuliiru prohibit post-verbal subject argument focus** and require a **cleft construction**:\n     - **Digo (Ex. 22, p. 388)**: `Ndipho atu achimanya kukala [iye]_FOCUS [ndi=ye ariyehenda mambo higo]_PRESUPPOSITION` ('That is when people knew that [it was] he [who did these things]').\n4. **Parameter 3: VS vs. SV Thetic (All-New / Presentational) Sentences (§4, Ex. 23–30, pp. 389–393)**:\n   - **Digo (VS-Only Thetic Sentences, Ex. 25, p. 390)**: In Digo, thetic sentences always have **VS constituent order** (or locative Class 16/17/18 existential agreement on the verb): `Hipho kare... Achiuka mwanache mmwenga achenda mwituni kutsonga ngoma. Phahi rero, chisha chindi, [ratuluka] [fisi], rina chitswa dza cha mutu, rina mikono minane na magulu manane, rina m’yo wa chuma`.\n   - **Suba-Simbiti (SV Thetic Sentences Allowed Both Initially & Medially, Ex. 29–30, pp. 391–392)**: Contrasts with Suba-Simbiti, which allows SV order in thetic sentences both at the start of a narrative (`[Musimbëtë na Mohaasha]_S m=bhaana abha enda ëmwë [bha-a-rë]_V`, Ex. 29) and within a narrative (`Bhoono ibhoora ryabo iryo ryaheerë, ko bhoorë bhu, [eng’era]_S [ekaasha]_V mu-rihisho irya waabho riyo`, Ex. 30).",
